roduct is zero. Let's denote the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a} + k \\mathbf{b}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a} - k \\mathbf{b}\\) by \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) respectively. That is, \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{a} + k \\mathbf{b}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{a} - k \\mathbf{b}\\).\n\nThe dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{a} + k \\mathbf{b}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} - k \\mathbf{b})\n\\]\nUsing the distributive property of the dot product, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} - k \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} + k \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} - k^2 \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\nSince the dot product is commutative, \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\), the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} - k^2 \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\|\\mathbf{a}\\| = 3\\) and \\(\\|\\mathbf{b}\\| = 4\\), so \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\|^2 = 9\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\|\\mathbf{b}\\|^2 = 16\\). Substituting these values in, we get:\n\\[\n9 - 16k^2\n\\]\nFor the vectors to be orthogonal, their dot product must be zero:\n\\[\n9 - 16k^2 = 0\n\\]\nSolving for \\( k \\), we get:\n\\[\n16k^2 = 9 \\implies k^2 = \\frac{9}{16} \\implies k = \\pm \\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\nThus, the values of \\( k \\) that satisfy the condition are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pm \\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\pm \\frac{3}{4}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:317"}
